# Target C-II — parameterized near-full density of deep grid-locked force cells

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only quantitative strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** no-fixed-margin-TB1 side of the canonical high-`t` branch through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** force-carrying robust angle-density tradeoff; linear joint-cost budget; non-antipodal TB2 direction localization; C67 direction separation; bilateral birth loading; deep two-sided cusp argument; common-grid locking theorem; source-wide diameter-degree bound  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_parameterized_deep_grid_force_density_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The existing deep-cell theorem fixed one conservative angle and then spent half of the surviving force density to clear a prescribed exact-phase neighborhood. That was sufficient for a qualitative growing-depth theorem, but it hides a stronger quantitative fact.

On the no-fixed-margin-TB1 branch, the force-pair angle, separator loss, and two-sided axial margin are independent parameters. For every fixed positive choice of those parameters, a positive linear family survives. Letting the three fixed losses tend to zero shows that the density of deep grid-locked two-sided force cells can be made arbitrarily close to the full fixed-good density

\[
c_0={113\over20{,}000{,}000}=5.65\times10^{-6}.
\tag{PD.1}
\]

This also improves the coefficient in the disjoint deep-force braid by a factor approaching eight relative to the first fixed-constant extraction.

Put

\[
c={113\over5{,}000{,}000}=4c_0,
\qquad C=1{,}109{,}000,
\qquad H=4C=4{,}436{,}000.
\tag{PD.2}
\]

Fix constants

\[
0<\rho<{c_0\over6},
\qquad \eta>0,
\qquad \delta>0,
\tag{PD.3}
\]

such that

\[
\boxed{
\lambda:=c_0-6\rho-\eta-2\delta>0.
}
\tag{PD.4}
\]

For an exact-phase radius `r>=0`, write

\[
m_r:=2r+1
\tag{PD.5}
\]

and choose any fixed threshold

\[
\boxed{
T_r\ge4,
\qquad
{m_rc\over T_r}<\eta.
}
\tag{PD.6}
\]

For example, `T_r=1+max(4,m_r c/eta)` is enough after harmless upward rounding.

---

## Theorem PD — parameterized deep grid-force density

Assume the positive-density fixed-margin TB1 alternative does not occur. Then, for every fixed `r` and every fixed parameter triple satisfying (PD.3)--(PD.4), all sufficiently large `D` contain a family

\[
Q_D(r;\rho,\eta,\delta)
\tag{PD.7}
\]

of interface centers with

\[
\boxed{
|Q_D(r;\rho,\eta,\delta)|
>\lambda D-o(D).
}
\tag{PD.8}
\]

Every selected center `i` simultaneously has the following properties.

### 1. Deep exact phase

Every interface

\[
j\in[i-r,i+r]
\tag{PD.9}
\]

is exact-antipodal and `T_r`-good:

\[
J_j<T_rC.
\tag{PD.10}
\]

Hence all rows in the neighborhood have exactly one phase,

\[
u_{i-r}=u_{i-r+1}=\cdots=u_{i+r+1}=:v_i,
\tag{PD.11}
\]

and

\[
N_j,L_j<T_rC,
\quad
B_j<{T_rC\over D},
\quad
S_j+S_{j+1}<T_rC,
\quad
0<|G_j|<T_rC.
\tag{PD.12}
\]

### 2. One common inverse-`D` train grid

There is one column family `B_0`, independent of the center and of `r`, with

\[
|B_0|>0.0783D-o(D),
\tag{PD.13}
\]

such that every row in every selected neighborhood crosses every `b in B_0` in common order and

\[
\boxed{
0<\theta(\tau_j,b)-{\pi\over3}
<{31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{PD.14}
\]

All selected neighborhood row axes lie in the same `O(D^{-1})` projective window, and the `B_0` axes lie in their own `O(D^{-1})` window.

### 3. `rho`-wide exact force diamond

At the center,

\[
G_i
=D(\lambda_i^-d_i^-+\lambda_i^+d_i^+),
\qquad \lambda_i^\pm>0,
\tag{PD.15}
\]

for two actual switching diameter bars whose projective gap is at least `rho`. Because the center comes from the original fixed-good force family,

\[
|G_i|<H.
\tag{PD.16}
\]

The two bars have four distinct endpoints, cross centrally, and their four-endpoint hull contains the fixed-angle central disk from the wide-dipole theorem.

### 4. Bilateral birth

Except for only `O_rho(1)` interfaces over the whole shore, the two selected force bars have birth endpoints on opposite ordered host subarcs. Those bounded exceptions have already been deleted in (PD.8).

### 5. `delta`-two-sided transverse margin

Both selected force bars satisfy

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([d_i^\pm],[v_i])\ge\delta.
\tag{PD.17}
\]

Consequently the elementary antipodal dipole coordinate has the quantitative two-sided cusp

\[
\boxed{
|\ell_{h_i^\pm}(s d^{(i)})|
\ge2D|s|\sin\delta,
}
\tag{PD.18}
\]

with opposite signs, and the exact-force coefficients obey

\[
\boxed{
{|G_i|\over D}\sin\delta
\le \lambda_i^\pm
\le {H\over D\sin\rho}.
}
\tag{PD.19}
\]

Thus the geometric cusp remains uniformly conditioned even though the absolute KKT scale may vanish.

---

## 1. Robust exact-force density loses only `6 rho`

The parameterized force-carrying robust fork gives

\[
|E_D^{force}(\rho)|+|S_D(\rho)|
>(c_0-6\rho)D-o(D).
\tag{PD.20}
\]

If `S_D(rho)` had positive density for any fixed `rho>0`, that would itself be the positive-density fixed-margin TB1 alternative. Under the hypothesis of Theorem PD,

\[
|S_D(\rho)|=o(D),
\]

and therefore

\[
\boxed{
|E_D^{force}(\rho)|
>(c_0-6\rho)D-o(D).
}
\tag{PD.21}
\]

Every center in this family is an original fixed-good interface, so `J_i<H=4C`; in particular it is `T_r`-good whenever `T_r>=4`.

---

## 2. A radius-`r` separator neighborhood costs only `eta D`

Call an interface `T_r`-bad when `J_i>=T_rC`. The global joint-cost budget

\[
\sum_iJ_i<CcD+o(D)
\tag{PD.22}
\]

implies

\[
|Z_D^{bad}(T_r)|<{c\over T_r}D+o(D).
\tag{PD.23}
\]

At fixed `T_r`, the good non-antipodal TB2 population is `o(D)` by the common-column direction-packing argument. The good TB1 population is also `o(D)` under the no-fixed-margin-TB1 hypothesis. Hence the full separator set

\[
Z_D(T_r)
\tag{PD.24}
\]

of threshold-bad or non-antipodal interfaces obeys

\[
|Z_D(T_r)|<{c\over T_r}D+o(D).
\tag{PD.25}
\]

A separator can lie in the radius-`r` window of at most `m_r=2r+1` possible centers. Therefore the number of centers whose neighborhood meets a separator is at most

\[
m_r|Z_D(T_r)|
<{m_rc\over T_r}D+o(D)
<\eta D+o(D).
\tag{PD.26}
\]

Deleting those centers from (PD.21) leaves

\[
>(c_0-6\rho-\eta)D-o(D)
\tag{PD.27}
\]

`rho`-wide exact-force centers with clean radius-`r` exact-phase neighborhoods.

---

## 3. Bilateral birth costs only `O(1)`

The fixed-angle bilateral theorem gives

\[
N_{same}(D,\rho)=O_\rho(1).
\tag{PD.28}
\]

Delete those interfaces. This does not alter the linear coefficient in (PD.27).

---

## 4. The two-sided axial margin costs only `2 delta D`

All center row axes lie in the same shrinking projective window. Choose one selected force bar from each center that has at least one force ray within `delta` of its local reaction axis.

Switching supports at distinct interfaces are disjoint, so the chosen diameter bars are distinct. Their directions lie in the `delta`-neighborhood of the common row-axis window, whose projective length is

\[
2\delta+o(1).
\tag{PD.29}
\]

C67 separates distinct actual diameter directions by at least `1/D`. Therefore the number of such axis-near centers is at most

\[
\boxed{
2\delta D+o(D).
}
\tag{PD.30}
\]

Delete them from (PD.27). The retained count is precisely

\[
(c_0-6\rho-\eta-2\delta)D-o(D)
=\lambda D-o(D),
\]

proving (PD.8) and (PD.17).

The derivative and force-coefficient bounds (PD.18)--(PD.19) follow exactly as in the fixed-constant two-sided theorem.

---

## 5. Common-grid locking is free

The common-grid theorem chooses `B_0` once from the global high-`t` complete rectangle. Every original fixed-good robust center crosses all of `B_0` with the inverse-`D` angle bound (PD.14).

Inside a clean neighborhood all row phases are exactly equal to the center phase. Hence every row in (PD.11) inherits the same `B_0` crossings and the same angle bound with no factor depending on `r`.

Thus none of the density deletions above spends any additional column density or phase accuracy.

---

## 6. Arbitrarily high retained density

The parameters are fixed before sending `D->infinity`, but they may be chosen as small positive constants. Therefore:

### Corollary PD.1

For every fixed `epsilon` with

\[
0<\epsilon<c_0,
\tag{PD.31}
\]

one may choose positive fixed `rho,eta,delta` satisfying

\[
6\rho+\eta+2\delta<\epsilon
\tag{PD.32}
\]

so that, for every fixed `r`, the no-TB1 branch contains more than

\[
\boxed{
(c_0-\epsilon)D-o(D)
}
\tag{PD.33}
\]

deep bilateral two-sided exact-force centers, all locked to the same `B_0` grid.

The geometric angle/cusp margins depend on `epsilon` but remain strictly positive constants independent of `D`.

An explicit convenient choice retaining ninety percent of `c_0` is

\[
\rho=\delta={c_0\over100},
\qquad
\eta={c_0\over50}.
\tag{PD.34}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
\lambda={9c_0\over10}
={1017\over200{,}000{,}000}
=5.085\times10^{-6},
}
\tag{PD.35}
\]

and one may take

\[
T_r=201(2r+1),
\tag{PD.36}
\]

because `m_r c/T_r<c_0/50=eta`.

The resulting normalized two-sided squared-length cusp margin is

\[
2\sin(c_0/100)>0.
\tag{PD.37}
\]

---

## 7. Growing-depth and optimized disjoint-braid corollaries

The leading coefficient in (PD.8) is independent of every fixed radius. A diagonal extraction therefore gives radii `r_D->infinity` with the same density `lambda D-o(D)` while choosing `D` fast enough that

\[
{T_{r_D}\over D}\to0.
\tag{PD.38}
\]

Thus all normalized local switching masses and reactions in the selected growing neighborhoods tend to zero.

Apply the simultaneous window/endpoint conflict graph from the disjoint-braid theorem. Its maximum degree is

\[
4\Delta(H)+4r_D,
\qquad
\Delta(H)\le(4\,3^{1/8}+o(1))D^{3/4}.
\tag{PD.39}
\]

Choose the diagonal also with `r_D=o(D^{3/4})`. Then there is a pairwise window-disjoint and force-endpoint-disjoint family of size

\[
\boxed{
\left(
{\lambda\over16\,3^{1/8}}-o(1)
\right)D^{1/4}.
}
\tag{PD.40}

All cross-cell selected diameter bars cross geometrically, every cell uses the same linear `B_0` column family, and every cell retains its `rho`/`delta` quantitative force and cusp margins.

For the ninety-percent choice (PD.34), the explicit leading braid coefficient is

\[
\boxed{
{0.9c_0\over16\,3^{1/8}}
\approx2.77032561594\times10^{-7}.
}
\tag{PD.41}

In the parameter limit `rho,eta,delta downarrow0`, the attainable leading coefficient approaches

\[
\boxed{
{c_0\over16\,3^{1/8}}
={c\over64\,3^{1/8}}
\approx3.07813957327\times10^{-7}.
}
\tag{PD.42}

This is eight times the leading coefficient of the initial fixed-constant `c/32` braid extraction.

---

## 8. Frontier effect

The no-fixed-margin-TB1 branch cannot explain the robust population by saying that deep, bilateral, two-sided, grid-locked force cells are sparse. Up to an arbitrarily small fixed density loss, **every** interface in the original fixed-good lower-bound population can be required to have that full structure.

For any prescribed finite exact-phase depth and any prescribed small density loss, the survivor contains

```text
(c0-o_epsilon(1)) D deep force centers
+ one common >0.0783 D near-triangular column family
+ bilateral ordered birth
+ fixed positive force-pair angle
+ fixed positive two-sided transverse margin
+ uniform first-order cusp
+ bounded-complexity exact-phase neighborhoods.
```

On a growing-depth diagonal, an optimized `Omega(D^(1/4))` subfamily can additionally be made window-disjoint and force-endpoint-disjoint.

The remaining closure issue is therefore not lack of density. It is to prove that this near-maximal population is incompatible with the shared ordered near-triangular grid and source diameter geometry, or to turn that incompatibility into a strict diameter descent or an exact unit triangle.

No C-II or Level-I closure is claimed.

**Disposition:** parameterized the deep grid-force extraction, retaining arbitrarily close to the full `c_0D` fixed-good density in the no-TB1 branch and improving the disjoint deep-force braid coefficient up to the sharp limit allowed by the present density input. C-II and Level I remain open.
